{"reasoning":"To design a compliant cloud architecture, I must incorporate the critical constraints specified by the Orchestrator, namely data residency strictly within the EU region, encryption at rest with AES-256 GCM using customer-managed keys, and enforcing a strict key rotation every 90 days. These constraints dictate the choice of cloud regions, encryption services, and key management policies.","findings":"1. Data Storage: All data storage services (e.g., object storage, databases) must be provisioned exclusively in the EU region (e.g., AWS EU (Frankfurt), Azure West Europe, or GCP Europe-West). No replication or backups should cross outside the EU.\n\n2. Encryption: Data at rest must be encrypted using AES-256 GCM. This encryption must utilize customer-managed keys (CMK) to allow control over key lifecycle.\n\n3. Key Management: Use a cloud-native key management service (e.g., AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault, GCP KMS) configured with customer-managed keys.\n\n4. Key Rotation: Enforce an automated key rotation policy that rotates keys exactly every 90 days, no exceptions.\n\n5. Compliance Documentation: Include these requirements explicitly in the architecture diagrams, technical specifications, and implementation guides to ensure the Lead Engineer and implementation teams follow them strictly.\n\n6. Monitoring and Auditing: Integrate monitoring to verify compliance with data residency and key rotation frequency, with alerts for any deviations.\n\nThis approach ensures all compliance mandates are embedded into the architecture and operational procedures.","decision":"Design the cloud architecture with all data storage located in the EU region, encrypt all data at rest using AES-256 GCM with customer-managed keys managed via a cloud KMS, and enforce an automated 90-day key rotation schedule. Document these constraints explicitly for the Lead Engineer and ensure monitoring is in place to maintain compliance throughout the data migration project.","confidence":0.95}

